from chemistry_qa_writer import q,write_bank
from chemistry_helpers import TOPICS
items=[
q(1,'Define an element.','A substance containing atoms with the same number of protons.','4.1.1.1'),
q(1,'What is the atomic number of an atom?','Its number of protons.','4.1.1.4'),
q(1,'Name the particle with no charge and relative mass 1.','Neutron.','4.1.1.4','2019 Q04.1'),
q(1,'Which separation method recovers an insoluble solid from a liquid?','Filtration.','4.1.1.2'),
q(1,'Give the electron arrangement of calcium, atomic number 20.','2,8,8,2.','4.1.1.7','2022 Q01.4'),
q(1,'How does boiling point change down Group 0?','It increases.','4.1.2.4','2022 Q05.4'),
q(2,'Explain the difference between a compound and a mixture.','- In a compound, different elements are **chemically combined in fixed proportions**.\n- In a mixture, substances are **not chemically combined** and can be separated by physical methods.','4.1.1.1–4.1.1.2'),
q(2,'Explain why a neutral atom has no overall charge.','- It has **equal numbers of protons and electrons**.\n- Their equal but opposite charges **cancel**.','4.1.1.4'),
q(2,'Explain why chlorine-35 and chlorine-37 are isotopes of the same element.','- They have the **same number of protons**.\n- They have **different numbers of neutrons**.','4.1.1.5','2019 Q04.3'),
q(2,'A sodium atom loses one electron. Explain why the resulting ion is positive and why it is still sodium.','- It now has one more positive proton than negative electrons, giving a **+1 charge**.\n- The number of **protons is unchanged**, so its identity is unchanged.','4.1.1.4'),
q(2,'Explain why helium is unreactive even though it has only two electrons.','- Its first electron shell is **full**.\n- This is a **stable arrangement**, so it does not readily gain, lose or share electrons.','4.1.2.4','2023 Q08.1'),
q(2,'Why did Mendeleev sometimes place elements out of strict atomic-weight order?','- He wanted elements with **similar chemical properties in the same group**.\n- Strict atomic-weight order sometimes put elements in **inappropriate groups**.','4.1.2.2','2019 Q01.1; 2023 Q01.4'),
q(2,'Give two physical differences between a typical transition metal and a Group 1 metal.','- A transition metal generally has a **higher melting point**.\n- It generally has a **higher density**.','4.1.3.1','2022 Q01.3'),
q(2,'Describe two features of the plum pudding model.','- A **ball of positive charge**.\n- **Negative electrons embedded** within it; no central nucleus.','4.1.1.3','2023 Q01.1'),
q(3,'An aluminium-27 ion has atomic number 13 and charge 3+. Calculate its numbers of protons, neutrons and electrons.','- Protons = **13**.\n- Neutrons = 27 − 13 = **14**.\n- Electrons = 13 − 3 = **10**.','4.1.1.5','2019 Q04.1–04.3'),
q(3,'An element contains 60% isotope of mass 10 and 40% isotope of mass 11. Calculate its relative atomic mass.','- Weighted total = (60 × 10) + (40 × 11) = **1,040**.\n- Divide by the total percentage: 1,040 ÷ 100.\n- **Aᵣ = 10.4**, with no unit.','4.1.1.6','2022 Q05.5; 2023 Q01.5'),
q(3,'Explain why potassium is more reactive than sodium.','- Its outer electron is **farther from the nucleus**, with more occupied shells.\n- Increased **shielding and distance reduce the attraction** to the outer electron.\n- The electron is **lost more easily**, so potassium reacts more readily.','4.1.2.5','2022 Q05.2'),
q(3,'Explain why chlorine is more reactive than bromine.','- Chlorine has **fewer occupied electron shells**.\n- There is less distance/shielding, giving a **stronger nuclear attraction for an incoming electron**.\n- Chlorine gains an electron more readily.','4.1.2.6'),
q(3,'Write a balanced equation for sodium reacting with water and state what makes the final solution alkaline.','- **2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂**.\n- The product **sodium hydroxide** supplies hydroxide ions in solution, making it alkaline.','4.1.2.5','2022 Q05.3'),
q(3,'Explain how later discoveries supported Mendeleev’s periodic table.','- He left **gaps** for undiscovered elements.\n- He **predicted their properties** from surrounding elements.\n- Newly discovered elements had properties that **matched the predictions**, supporting the table.','4.1.2.2','2019 Q01.2'),
q(3,'A typical atom has radius 0.10 nm. Convert this to metres and compare it with a nucleus of radius 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ m.','- 0.10 × 10⁻⁹ = **1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ m**.\n- Ratio = 10⁻¹⁰ ÷ 10⁻¹⁴ = **10⁴**.\n- The atom’s radius is **10,000 times** the nuclear radius.','4.1.1.5'),
q(4,'Chlorine is added to potassium bromide solution. Write the balanced full and ionic equations and explain why the reaction occurs.','- Full: **Cl₂ + 2KBr → 2KCl + Br₂**.\n- Ionic: **Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂**.\n- Chlorine is **more reactive than bromine**, so it displaces bromine from bromide.\n- Potassium ions are **spectator ions** and do not change.','4.1.2.6'),
q(4,'Explain four choices made when separating ink by paper chromatography.','- Use **pencil** for the baseline because it does not dissolve and separate like ink.\n- Keep the baseline **above the solvent level** so the sample is not washed into the solvent.\n- Use small spots so components begin in a narrow region and can separate clearly.\n- Mark the **solvent front promptly** before evaporation makes its position unclear.','4.1.1.2'),
q(4,'Compare simple and fractional distillation and give an example of each.','- Both **evaporate a liquid and condense its vapour** for collection.\n- Simple distillation recovers a solvent, such as **water from salt solution**.\n- Fractional distillation separates miscible liquids, such as **ethanol and water**, using their different boiling points.\n- A fractionating column provides **repeated evaporation and condensation**, improving separation.','4.1.1.2'),
q(6,'Describe how to obtain dry sand and dry salt crystals from a mixture of sand and salt. Explain the separation at each stage.','- Add water and stir: **salt dissolves**, but sand remains insoluble.\n- **Filter** the mixture; sand is the residue and salt solution is the filtrate.\n- Rinse the sand with a little water to remove remaining dissolved salt, then dry it.\n- Gently heat the filtrate to **evaporate some water** and concentrate the solution.\n- Leave it to cool so **salt crystals form**, then filter and dry the crystals.\n- Wear goggles and handle hot equipment carefully. These are physical separations; no new substances are produced.','4.1.1.2'),
q(6,'Explain how alpha-particle scattering changed the model of the atom, and describe two later developments.','- The plum pudding model spread positive charge throughout the atom, with embedded electrons.\n- Most alpha particles passed straight through foil, showing the atom is **mostly empty space**.\n- Some positive alpha particles deflected, showing a concentrated **positive charge** repelled them.\n- Very few scattered backwards, supporting a **tiny, dense nucleus containing most mass**, rather than a spread-out positive sphere.\n- **Bohr** proposed electrons at specific energy levels or shells.\n- **Chadwick** later provided evidence for neutrons within the nucleus.','4.1.1.3','2019 Q04.4; 2023 Q01.1–01.2'),
q(6,'Compare Group 1 and Group 7 in terms of outer electrons, ion formation and the change in reactivity down each group.','- Group 1 atoms have **one outer electron** and lose it to form **+1 ions**.\n- Group 7 atoms have **seven outer electrons** and gain one to form **−1 ions**.\n- Down both groups, outer shells are farther from the nucleus and more shielded.\n- In Group 1 this weakens attraction to the outer electron, so it is **lost more easily**.\n- Group 1 reactivity therefore **increases** down the group.\n- In Group 7 attraction for an incoming electron becomes weaker, so gain is less easy and reactivity **decreases**.','4.1.2.5–4.1.2.6','2022 Q05.1–05.3')]
write_bank(TOPICS[0],'Atomic structure and the periodic table',items,'- Atomic models and particles: 2019 Q04.1–04.4; 2023 Q01.1–01.2.\n- Isotope averages: 2022 Q05.5; 2023 Q01.5.\n- Periodic table development: 2019 Q01.1–01.2; 2023 Q01.4.\n- Group properties: 2019 Q01.4–01.6; 2022 Q05.1–05.4; 2023 Q08.1.\n\nThese themes recur in the three-paper sample. Separation and other coverage questions are additional specification-based practice; the prompts, values and allocations here are original.')
